      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first people in the area were Native Americans who hunted and fished along the White River before settlers arrived.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.arkansasheritage.com/blog/dah/2023/01/14/delta-roads-and-travels---indian-bay",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By the 1850s, the community was known as Indian Bay and already hosted several businesses, including a cotton gin.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.arkansasheritage.com/blog/dah/2023/01/14/delta-roads-and-travels---indian-bay",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the mid‑19th century, Indian Bay boasted stores, a cotton gin, a sawmill, and became a key steamboat stop on the White River.",
